# Security control bootstrap SOP

Repository allowlist: `${repository}`. Default branch: `${default_branch}`. Authorized automation identity: `${github_app_login}`.

1. Confirm `repository_full_name` exactly matches the allowlist, the PR is open and same-repository, its base is `${default_branch}`, it has the `demo-application` label, it is not labeled `demo-reset`, and `DEMO_STATE` is not `resetting`.
2. Capture the application PR head SHA and require it to equal input `head_sha`.
3. Inspect `.github/workflows` and repository rulesets for `security-gate`. If both reviewed controls and requirement already exist, comment that no change was needed and stop successfully.
4. Read `demo/control-templates/manifest.json`. Recalculate every source SHA-256 and require equality with the manifest.
5. Create `security-controls/<application-pr-number>-<short-head-sha>` from the current `${default_branch}`. If precisely that expected controls PR is already open, do not create a duplicate: capture its controls head SHA, re-read and validate its identity, base, allowlisted diff, and manifest digest, then require that controls head SHA to remain unchanged immediately before its compare-and-swap merge at step 8. The controls head must not be compared to the `${default_branch}` SHA. Build and validate the local commit before creating a missing remote branch, then create the remote branch with a single non-force push. The GitHub integration executes each shell command in a fresh POSIX `/bin/sh` process: define every captured variable (including the `${default_branch}` SHA) in each command that uses it, and use only POSIX shell constructs—no Bash here-strings, arrays, `mapfile`, or Bash parameter substitution. After confirming the expected remote branch is absent, reset only the matching local branch to the captured `${default_branch}` SHA (for example, `git switch -C`) instead of using `git checkout -b`; this is not a force push. Stage only the allowlisted destination before validating the exact one-file change, then inspect `git diff --cached --name-only`; ordinary `git diff` does not include a new untracked file. If a prior interrupted run left precisely that expected branch pointing unchanged at the captured `${default_branch}` SHA and there is no controls PR for it, delete only that empty branch and retry once; otherwise stop. Copy each manifest source byte-for-byte to its destination. Open one PR targeting `${default_branch}`.
6. Re-read the controls PR. Require author `${github_app_login}`, expected base, expected branch prefix, exact allowlisted destinations, exact digests, no additional files, no binaries, no symlinks, no submodules, and unchanged controls head SHA.
7. Wait for required validation checks. Stop if any required check is not successful.
8. Merge the controls PR using its validated head SHA. Do not approve it and do not use a force operation.
9. Verify destinations on `${default_branch}`. Create or update a dedicated ruleset named `talkdesk-demo-security-gate` requiring `security-gate`; grant `${github_app_login}` bypass for pull requests only. Store its numeric ID in repository variable `DEMO_SECURITY_RULESET_ID`.
10. Comment once on the original application PR with the controls PR URL, controls merge SHA, installed destinations, ruleset/check name, and that the branch will now be updated.
11. Only after the comment succeeds, call GitHub update-branch for the application PR with its captured SHA as `expected_head_sha`.
12. Verify the application head SHA changed and a workflow named `Security gate` started for that SHA. Comment and stop on conflicts or concurrent changes.
